\name{query_gene_database}
\alias{query_gene_database}
\title{Query gene associations from online databases}
\description{
Retrieve gene associations for a disease or phenotype from supported databases.
The function is designed so that additional data sources can be added in the
future. Currently, the Open Targets Platform is supported.
}
\usage{
query_gene_database(disease, database = c("open_targets"), limit = 100,
  request_fun = NULL)
}
\arguments{
\item{disease}{A non-empty character string describing the disease or phenotype
of interest. The value is matched against entries in the selected database.}

\item{database}{A character string identifying the database to query. Defaults
to \code{"open_targets"}. Additional databases may be supported in future
versions.}

\item{limit}{A positive integer giving the maximum number of gene associations
to return.}

\item{request_fun}{Optional function used to perform HTTP requests. This
argument is primarily intended for testing; it should accept a GraphQL query
string and a named list of variables and must return the parsed content of the
\code{data} field.}
}
\value{
A tibble with one row per gene target containing the matched disease identifier
and name, the Open Targets target identifiers, the overall association score, a
list column of data-source specific scores, and a set of columns for each
reported evidence data type score.
}
\examples{
\donttest{
# Retrieve the top 25 genes associated with atrial fibrillation
genes <- query_gene_database("atrial fibrillation", limit = 25)
}
}
